# What a type REACHES, asked once (panel 149, 2026-09-14; `docs/work/DEFECTS.md`
# 033 and 035).
#
# A handle is C's pointer to something opaque, and a type reaches one when the
# handle is the type itself, or sits in a field, or in a field of a field, or as
# the element of a fixed array, or in a variant's payload. This walk answers
# **where**: absent when nothing is reached, the empty string when the type IS a
# handle, and otherwise the dotted path to it — `Font.recs`, `Outer.p.s`.
#
# **THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE THE WALK WAS ALREADY WRITTEN AND ALREADY HAD A
# SECOND CALLER COMING.** It lived in `check/map_keys.hero`, where the rule that
# refuses a handle as a map key needs it. Panel 149 was convened on whether
# `unmarked_handle_producer` should look inside a returned record, and the
# compiler-engineer seat found the walk already shipping, with three tests and a
# golden printing its answer. CLAUDE.md's opening rule is that each rule is
# written in exactly one place and everything else cites it, so the walk moved
# here rather than being asked a second time somewhere else. The FFI half of
# this compiler has paid for the alternative: `check/table.hero` records
# `c_type_of` and `c_type` disagreeing about a group's record for a whole
# milestone, in silence.
#
# **Why here and not in `handles.hero`, which is where a question about a handle
# is asked once.** Measured at the sitting: every function in that module takes
# an `ast.Decl` or loose pieces and it imports no table, while this walk needs
# `state.Checker` and `check/table`. Twelve modules `use handles`, and three of
# them are the parser, the mutator and the emitters. Dragging the checker's
# interned table into `handles.hero` would drag it into the parser.
#
# ## Termination, and it is the reason a depth bound is not here
#
# The walk crosses two structures and only one of them is safe on its own.
#
# **The interned type table is acyclic by construction**: a structure is
# interned after its components, so a component's id is smaller than its
# container's. `check/ffi.hero`'s `crosses_the_boundary` writes the same
# argument down for the same reason.
#
# **The declaration graph is NOT acyclic, and a user can write the cycle.**
# `record Loop` holding a `Loop`, or two records holding each other, both parse
# and both pass `ffi_field`; `check/sized.hero` refuses them — and **does not
# stop this pass**. Measured 2026-09-14: a program carrying both a record cycle
# and an unmarked producer prints `error[no_size]` AND
# `error[unmarked_handle_producer]`, because `checker.hero` pushes `sized`'s
# diagnostics and then calls the later rules anyway. So an unguarded walk over
# declarations would not terminate on a program that compiles today.
#
# **The guard is a set of the declarations already on the route**, and it is
# sound for this question because whether a type reaches a handle is a property
# of the type and not of the path that arrived at it: a declaration that reached
# nothing the first time reaches nothing the second.
#
# **What was here before was `if depth > 16`, and that was defect 035.** It
# terminated by GIVING UP, silently, so a handle used as a map key was refused at
# one nesting depth and accepted at the next. The sentence licensing that bound
# still sits beside the float twin in `check/map_keys.hero`, where it is true —
# a missed float refusal costs a named abort because the runtime guard is still
# behind it. It is false here: behind the handle rule there is no runtime guard,
# only an address, and `handles.hero` calls the refusal it protects *a hole
# nothing can close*. **The walk was copied and its premise was left behind on
# the original.**

use ast
use source
use check/table
use check/state
use handles

# What the walk found: WHICH handle, and WHERE. Two callers want different
# halves and one walk answers both, which is the whole reason this record exists
# rather than two functions. `check/map_keys.hero` prints the path; the
# completeness rule in `check/acquiring.hero` keys a map on the declaration,
# because `one_tag_one_type` makes a handle's declaration a program-wide
# identity.
record Reached
    # The handle's own declaration, which is what identifies the handle TYPE.
    decl: i64
    # The dotted route to it, empty when the type asked about IS the handle.
    path: str

# Where this type reaches a handle. Absent when it reaches none, a `path` of
# `""` when the type IS one, and otherwise the dotted path anchored at the first
# declaration on the route: `Font.recs`, `Outer.p.s`.
function reaches_handle(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64) -> Reached?
    seen: {i64: bool} @ {}

    return walk(c, s, decls, ty: ty, anchor: true, @seen)

# The walk. `anchor` decides whether this level writes the declaration's own
# name in front of the path, and only the outermost level does: the reader wants
# `Outer.p.s` and not `Outer.p.Pair.s`, so the name is written once, where the
# route begins. It is a fact about the MESSAGE and not about the world, which is
# why it is a parameter rather than a second walk.
function walk(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], ty: i64, anchor: bool, @seen: {i64: bool}) -> Reached?
    match table.get(c.out.types, ty)
        .named n =>
            if handles.is_handle(decls[n.decl])
                return ok(Reached(decl: n.decl, path: ""))

            # Already on this route: a cycle, or the second arm of a diamond.
            # Either way the answer for this declaration is settled.
            if !seen[n.decl].is_err()
                return fail(code: "absent", msg: "this declaration is already on the route")
            seen[n.decl] @ true
            inner = below(c, s, decls, decl: n.decl, @seen)

            if inner.is_err()
                return inner

            if !anchor
                return inner

            return ok(
                Reached(decl: inner.must().decl, path: source.span_text(s, decls[n.decl].name) + "." + inner.must().path)
            )
        .fixed x    => return walk(c, s, decls, ty: x.element, anchor: anchor, @seen)
        .array arr  => return walk(c, s, decls, ty: arr.element, anchor: anchor, @seen)
        .fallible o => return walk(c, s, decls, ty: o.payload, anchor: anchor, @seen)
        .map m =>
            in_key = walk(c, s, decls, ty: m.key, anchor: anchor, @seen)

            if !in_key.is_err()
                return in_key

            return walk(c, s, decls, ty: m.value, anchor: anchor, @seen)
        .int_ty | .float_ty | .bool | .str | .ptr | .cstr | .unit => return fail(code: "absent", msg: "no handle here")
        .function_ty | .case_ty | .failure | .generic | .error    => return fail(code: "absent", msg: "no handle here")

    return fail(code: "absent", msg: "unreached")

# The field chain below one declaration, carrying no owner: `s`, or `p.s`. A
# variant answers for the first case payload that reaches one, because a value
# of that type may be in any case and the diagnostic needs a route the reader
# can follow.
function below(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], decl: i64, @seen: {i64: bool}) -> Reached?
    match decls[decl].kind
        .record_decl rec => return in_fields(c, s, decls, fields: rec.fields, @seen)
        .variant_decl v =>
            for one_case in v.cases
                found = in_fields(c, s, decls, fields: one_case.fields, @seen)

                if !found.is_err()
                    return found

            return fail(code: "absent", msg: "no payload reaches a handle")
        .constant_decl | .function_decl | .test_decl => return fail(code: "absent", msg: "not a data declaration")

    return fail(code: "absent", msg: "unreached")

# The first field that reaches a handle, as a dotted chain of field names. It
# asks `written_type` for `ffi_sweep`'s reason: the node is what the author
# wrote and the interned id is what the checker knows, and only the second can
# be asked what KIND of type it is.
function in_fields(c: state.Checker, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], fields: [ast.Field], @seen: {i64: bool}) -> Reached?
    for field in fields
        written = state.written_type(c.out, field.ty)

        if written.is_err()
            continue
        found = walk(c, s, decls, ty: written.must(), anchor: false, @seen)

        if found.is_err()
            continue
        name = source.span_text(s, field.name)

        if found.must().path == ""
            return ok(Reached(decl: found.must().decl, path: name))

        return ok(Reached(decl: found.must().decl, path: name + "." + found.must().path))

    return fail(code: "absent", msg: "every field is the program's own")
